{
  "image_name": "2ac2bac037c04cf5fc16ed3fa5a51824.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the reported benefits and proposed mechanism of FLASH radiotherapy compared with conventional radiation. At the top, several radiation types, including electrons, soft X-rays, protons, and carbon ions, are shown as capable of producing a FLASH effect in mouse models. The main visible message is that ultra-high dose rate treatment can better spare normal tissues while maintaining tumour control. Examples of protected organs include brain, lung, skin, intestine, and developing tissues, with notes suggesting reduced inflammation, less fibrosis or ulceration, preserved intestinal crypts and function, and protection of cognition or morphogenesis. Tumour irradiation is shown as iso-effective, with heavy ions also appearing to inhibit metastasis.\n\nThe lower part contrasts standard radiotherapy with FLASH. Conventional treatment is depicted as causing more DNA damage, which leads to vascular injury, depletion of stem and progenitor cells, and cell death. These changes trigger a broad inflammatory response involving multiple immune cell types and inflammatory mediators, ultimately reducing organ function. In the FLASH pathway, DNA damage appears reduced, vascular damage and cell death are lower, stem and progenitor cells are preserved, inflammation is dampened, and organ function is maintained.",
